::STAT_U01_L03_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is best for showing individual values of one numerical variable?
{
~A. Pie chart#A pie chart is for categorical parts of a whole.
~B. Two-way table#A two-way table organizes two categorical variables.
~C. Segmented bar graph#Segmented bars compare categorical relative frequencies.
=D. Dot plot#A dot plot preserves individual numerical values.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best shows the relationship between height and arm span?
{
=A. Scatterplot#Height and arm span are two numerical variables.
~B. Bar chart#A bar chart compares category counts.
~C. Histogram#A histogram shows one numerical variable.
~D. Pie chart#A pie chart shows categorical parts of a whole.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A histogram is most useful when the goal is to show what?
{
~A. The relationship between two categorical variables#That is a two-way table situation.
~B. The exact identity of every person#Histograms summarize groups and do not identify individuals.
=C. The distribution of one numerical variable grouped into intervals#Histograms show frequencies across numerical intervals.
~D. The percent of a whole for categories#That is a categorical display purpose.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A box plot is especially useful for comparing what features of numerical data?
{
~A. Category names and labels#Category labels are not summarized by quartiles.
~B. Two categorical variables#Two-way tables handle two categorical variables.
~C. Parts of a whole#Pie charts show parts of a whole.
=D. Median, quartiles, and spread#Box plots summarize center and spread.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A teacher has one numerical variable: quiz score. Which display pair could fit?
{
~A. Scatterplot or two-way table#A scatterplot needs two numerical variables, and a two-way table needs categories.
=B. Dot plot or histogram#Both show distributions of one numerical variable.
~C. Pie chart or bar chart#Those are categorical displays.
~D. Segmented bar graph or pie chart#Those show categorical proportions.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which choice explains why a scatterplot is not right for one list of wait times?
{
~A. Wait time is categorical#Wait time is numerical.
~B. Scatterplots cannot use numbers#Scatterplots use numerical pairs.
=C. A scatterplot requires two numerical variables#One list of wait times is univariate numerical data.
~D. All wait-time data must use a pie chart#Pie charts are for categorical parts of a whole.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which data situation best fits a histogram?
{
=A. Ages of 200 visitors grouped by intervals#Ages are numerical and can be grouped into intervals.
~B. Favorite exhibits at a museum#Favorite exhibit is categorical.
~C. Grade level and lunch choice#Those are categorical variables.
~D. Hours studied and test score#Two numerical variables fit a scatterplot.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display should be chosen for distance traveled and time spent traveling for each trip?
{
~A. Pie chart#A pie chart is categorical.
=B. Scatterplot#Distance and time are paired numerical variables.
~C. Frequency table of categories#The variables are numerical measurements.
~D. Bar chart of trip names#Trip names would not show the relationship between distance and time.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A dot plot is useful instead of a histogram when what matters?
{
~A. Comparing two categorical variables#That calls for a two-way table.
~B. Showing only percentages of categories#That calls for relative frequency or pie chart.
~C. Proving cause and effect#A dot plot does not prove causation.
=D. Seeing individual data values#Dot plots show each recorded numerical value.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which statement correctly matches display and purpose?
{
~A. Histogram: compare favorite colors#Favorite colors are categorical.
=B. Histogram: show a numerical distribution#A histogram displays one numerical variable in intervals.
~C. Scatterplot: show one category count#Scatterplots use two numerical variables.
~D. Box plot: compare category labels#Box plots summarize numerical data.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which variable would be appropriate for a box plot?
{
~A. Favorite sport#This is categorical.
~B. Bus route name#This is a label.
~C. Preferred school subject#This is categorical.
=D. Heights of students in inches#Height is numerical and can be summarized with quartiles.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is least appropriate for one numerical variable?
{
~A. Dot plot#Dot plots show one numerical variable.
=B. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables, not one numerical variable.
~C. Histogram#Histograms show one numerical variable.
~D. Box plot#Box plots summarize one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records temperature for each day. Which display could show distribution?
{
=A. Histogram#Temperatures are numerical values that can be grouped into intervals.
~B. Two-way table#There are not two categorical variables.
~C. Pie chart#Temperature is numerical, not categorical.
~D. Segmented bar graph#Segmented bars compare categorical relative frequencies.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Why should units be considered when representing numerical data?
{
~A. Units turn categories into numbers#Units describe measurements, not categories.
~B. Units determine the correct answer letter#Answer letters are assessment formatting, not data meaning.
=C. Units clarify what the numerical values measure#Units help the display communicate the context accurately.
~D. Units replace the need for a display#A display is still needed to show patterns.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which graph would best compare distributions of test scores for two classes using quartiles?
{
=A. Box plots#Side-by-side box plots compare centers and spreads.
~B. Pie charts#Pie charts show categorical parts of a whole.
~C. Scatterplots#Scatterplots show relationships between two numerical variables.
~D. Two-way tables#Two-way tables summarize categorical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A scatterplot should be used only when each case has what?
{
=A. Two numerical values#Each point in a scatterplot represents a pair of numerical values.
~B. One category label#A single category label does not create a scatterplot point.
~C. A count and a category only#A mixed pair may need another display, not a standard scatterplot.
~D. Four answer choices#Answer choices are assessment structure, not data structure.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display would best show the spread of delivery times?
{
~A. Pie chart#Pie charts show categorical parts of a whole.
~B. Bar chart for categories#Delivery times are numerical measurements.
~C. Two-way frequency table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables.
=D. Box plot#A box plot summarizes spread and center for numerical delivery times.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A student selects a histogram for favorite pet type. What is the error?
{
~A. Histograms cannot show frequencies#Histograms do show frequencies.
~B. Pet data can never be displayed#Pet type can be displayed using a bar chart or table.
=C. Favorite pet type is categorical, not numerical#Histograms require numerical intervals.
~D. The data has too many categories for any display#Multiple categories can be displayed.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which question asks for a relationship between two numerical variables?
{
~A. Which snack is most popular?#That is one categorical variable.
~B. What are the ages of students in class?#That is one numerical variable.
~C. Which club has the most members?#That is categorical count comparison.
=D. Does practice time relate to free-throw percentage?#Practice time and free-throw percentage are numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display choice is appropriate for one numerical variable with many values?
{
=A. Histogram#A histogram can group many numerical values into intervals.
~B. Pie chart#A pie chart is for categorical proportions.
~C. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables.
~D. Bar chart of labels#Labels are categorical, not raw numerical values.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
What is the first decision when choosing a numerical display?
{
~A. Whether the graph will use blue or red#Color does not determine the appropriate display.
~B. Whether the data has a title#A title is useful but not the first display decision.
=C. Whether there is one numerical variable or two#One variable suggests distribution displays, while two numerical variables suggest a relationship display.
~D. Whether the values are written in a table#The table format does not decide the best display by itself.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A line of best fit would most likely be connected to which display?
{
~A. Pie chart#Pie charts do not use lines of best fit.
=B. Scatterplot#A line of best fit can summarize a trend in paired numerical data.
~C. Frequency table#Frequency tables list counts, not numerical trends.
~D. Box plot#Box plots summarize distribution, not paired trends.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which numerical display shows every value as a mark above a number line?
{
=A. Dot plot#Dot plots place marks above numerical values.
~B. Two-way table#Two-way tables organize category pairs.
~C. Pie chart#Pie charts show categorical parts.
~D. Scatterplot#Scatterplots plot paired numerical values on two axes.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display would not preserve every individual value?
{
~A. Dot plot#Dot plots usually preserve individual values.
~B. Data table#A data table can list individual values.
=C. Histogram#A histogram groups values into intervals, so exact individual values may not be visible.
~D. Stem-and-leaf plot#A stem-and-leaf plot can preserve individual values.
}

::STAT_U01_L03_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L03_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which justification is strongest for using a scatterplot?
{
~A. The data use category names#Category names do not fit a scatterplot.
=B. The data pair two numerical measurements for each case#Scatterplots represent paired numerical data.
~C. The question asks for the most popular category#That calls for a categorical display.
~D. The data should look colorful#Visual style is not statistical justification.
}
